Note = {11th International Conference on Innovation in Urban and Regional
   Planning-INPUT, University of Catania, Catania, ITALY, SEP 08-10, 2021},
Abstract = {Slowtourism is a sustainableway of traveling: it is not based on the
   consumption of resources and at the same time is linked to the discovery
   of local places and traditions. Furthermore, it favors a harmonious and
   responsible development of the territory in contrast with over tourism.
   Within this scenario and in the wake of a tradition rooted in Europe,
   the paths have become one of the most successful declinations of slow
   tourism. In this sense, the paths are increasingly becoming an integral
   part of the portfolio of tourism products in Sardinia, together with
   other types of tourism related to boating, culture, archeology,
   villages, cycling, food and wine, nature. The aim of this work is to
   investigate the role of slow tourism in Sardinia, with particular
   reference to the qualitative/quantitative evaluation of the model of the
   path of Santa Barbara Sulcis-Iglesiente and its exportability in other
   local contexts to enhance landscapes through the ancient tangible and
   intangible of mine habitats. Furthermore, the authors analyze the main
   potentially replicable elements of the path of Santa Barbara also in
   order to define best practices for the planning of slow tourism
   associated with smart tourism.},
