Note = {CONF ON METHODOLOGICAL AND ETHICAL ISSUES IN CLINICAL TRIALS, LONDON,
   ENGLAND, JUN 27-28, 1991},
Organization = {LONDON SCH ECON CTR PHILOS NAT \& SOCIAL SCI; ROYAL STAT SOC, MED SECT;
   MIND ASSOC; WELCOME TRUST; CIBA GEIGY; ECON \& SOCIAL RES COUNCIL; ANAL
   COMM},
Abstract = {The current widespread practice of using p-values as the main means of
   assessing and reporting the results of clinical trials cannot be
   defended. Reasons for grave concern over the present situation range
   from the unsatisfactory nature of p-values themselves, their very common
   misunderstanding by statisticians as well as by clinicians and their
   serious distorting influence on our perception of the very nature of
   clinical trials. It is argued, however, that only by fully understanding
   the reasons why they have become so universally popular can we hope to
   change opinion and introduce more sensible ways of summarizing and
   reporting results. Some of the ways in which this might happen are
   discussed.},
