   Society-for-Industrial-and-Organizational-Psychology, SAN DIEGO, CA,
   APR-MAY -, 1996},
Organization = {Soc Ind \& Org Psychol},
Abstract = {The present study investigated the affective responses of 169 Hispanic
   and 196 African American participants to a simulated selection decision
   involving race-bared affirmative action. Type of implementation
   procedure and qualification of beneficiary were manipulated in the
   simulated scenario, and subjects' affective reactions, perceptions of
   fairness and competence, and feelings of stigmatization were measured.
   Strong effects were obtained for the recipient qualification and
   affirmative action procedure manipulations. Race differences (African
   American vs. Hispanic) on the majority of dependent variables were not
   significant. The present study demonstrated that as long as minority
   group members did not feel they were less qualified than other
   applicants, the negative consequences of being preferentially selected
   were muted.},
