Organization = {IEE; Assoc Bldg Engineers Assoc; NHS Estate; IHEEM; ASHRAE; IPEM;
   IMechE, Construct \& Bldg Serv Div},
Abstract = {The airborne route of transmission has long been considered as being of
   only limited importance in the epidemiology of hospital acquired
   (nosocomial) infection. Despite accumulating evidence that significant
   nosocomial pathogens such as Staphylococcus aureus and Pseudomonas
   aeruginosa are transmitted in this manner, there is much scepticism
   about the overall contribution of airborne microorganisms to nosocomial
   infection. This scepticism reflects the lack of data on the airborne
   microflora in hospitals - a topic which has received scant attention in
   recent years. This paper reviews the evidence for airborne nosocomial
   infection and explores how the perception that the airborne route of
   transmission is of limited importance might have arisen. Priorities for
   further research activities in this field are also outlined.},
