   Environment and Engineering (ICRMBEE), Shah Alam, MALAYSIA, NOV 08-09,
   2017},
Abstract = {Bandung City is home to 2.5 million inhabitants, some of which are
   living in slums and squatter. However, the terms conveying this type of
   housing is not adequate to describe that of Indonesian called as
   kampungkota. Several studies suggest various variables in constituting
   kampungkota qualitatively. This study delves to define kampungkota in a
   quantitative manner, using the characteristics of slums and squatter.
   The samples for this study are 151 villages (kelurahan) in Bandung City.
   Ordinary Least Squares, Geographically Weighted Regression, and Spatial
   Cluster and Outlier Analysis are employed. It is suggested that
   kampungkota may have distinguished variables regarding to its location.
   As kampungkota may be smaller than administrative area of kelurahan, it
   can develop beyond the jurisdiction of kelurahan, as indicated by the
   clustering pattern of kampungkota. Keywords: kampongkota, informal
   urbanism, housing system, Indonesia.},
