Note = {9th International Conference on Education and Educational Psychology
   (ICEEPSY), Athens, GREECE, OCT 02-05, 2018},
Abstract = {The text presents the results of the research based on findings of a
   university course during which university students - majoring in
   teaching German as A foreign language (FL) - expressed their opinions
   about the stories of raven mothers in FLT. Interesting stories of raven
   mothers stir emotions and help teachers to develop students' language
   skills. The course was carried out in winter term 2017 as an elective
   literary course with students reading the following books: Julia
   Franck's (2007) Die Mittagsfrau (The Blind Side of the Heart) and Rucken
   an Rucken (Back to Back), Peter Wawerzinek's (2010) Rabenliebe (Bad
   Love), Angelika Klussendorf's (2011) Das Madchen (The Girl). The aim of
   this course was, firstly, to make students look for arguments to justify
   the character's behaviour that the students tried to comprehend as the
   behaviour of raven mothers was sometimes incomprehensible; secondly, to
   encourage students to read literature as a resource for understanding
   life in totalitarian regimes that has again become a discussed issue in
   German prose after 2000; thirdly, to improve students' language skills
   and language areas while presenting, discussing and writing. This paper
   presents in practical examples the ways how a teacher can enrich FL
   lessons with a literary text since literature in FLT is an issue not to
   be neglected by institutions training future teachers of FLT. (C) 2019
   Published by Future Academy www.FutureAcademy.org.UK},
