   Wolverhampton, Wolverhampton, ENGLAND, MAY 09-12, 1999},
Organization = {Univ Wolverhampton, Sch Appl Sci; Univ Wolverhampton, Sch Engn; Univ
   Wolverhampton, Built Environm},
Abstract = {In terrestrial environments plants contribute to weathering through a
   range of direct and indirect processes. Recent work has focused on
   identifying and quantifying the weathering impacts of micro-organisms
   and lower plants on bare rock surfaces, with less study of the role of
   higher plants. Experiments carried out by Julius Sachs (1832-1897)
   provide a valuable methodology for comparative studies of the weathering
   impacts of plant roots. Maize, nasturtium, cucumber and bean seeds have
   been grown in quartz sand on polished blocks of marble and Portland
   Limestone for 12-21 days under controlled conditions. Microscope
   observations reveal the presence of clear root etchings on some samples.},
